  "name": "Question 12",
  "problem": "Which of the following sets is a subspace of ${R}^{2}?$\n\n- $A={\u27e8x,y\u27e9\\in {R}^{2}:{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 0}$\n- $B={\u27e8x,y\u27e9\\in {R}^{2}:x=\\frac{1}{2}y+2}$\n- $C={0}$",
  "worked_solution": "Let's analyze each of the subsets.\n\n- The set $A$ is a subspace. There is only one vector in ${R}^{2}$ that satisfies the equation ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 0,$ namely, the zero vector $0=\u27e80,0\u27e9.$ So, $A={0}$, which is a special subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- The set $B$ is not a subspace because it does not contain the zero vector. Indeed, $0=\u27e80,0\u27e9$ does not satisfy the equation $x=\\frac{1}{2}y+2$ since $0\\ne \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 0+2.$\n\n- The set $C$ is a subspace. This subset has only one element, the zero vector $0=\u27e80,0\u27e9,$ which is a special subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"",
